## 📚 5. Comparison of SET Operators

| Feature | UNION | UNION ALL | INTERSECT | EXCEPT |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Combines results | Yes | Yes | No | No |
| Finds common rows | No | No | Yes | No |
| Finds differences | No | No | No | Yes |
| Removes duplicate rows | Yes | No | Yes | Yes |
| Retains duplicates | No | Yes | No | No |

### Key Learning Points

**UNION:** Combines both result sets and removes duplicate rows.

**UNION ALL:** Combines both result sets and keeps duplicate rows.

**INTERSECT:** Returns only common rows from both result sets.

**EXCEPT:** Returns rows from the first result set that are missing from the second result set.

**Important SQL Rules:**

1. Both SELECT statements must return the same number of columns.
2. Corresponding columns must have compatible data types.
3. Columns must be in the same order.
4. Column names in the final result are based on the first SELECT statement.
5. EXCEPT and INTERSECT return distinct rows.
6. The order of SELECT statements matters when using EXCEPT.

